---
.title = "The relay: a peer without a socket",
.description = "A relay carries a browser peer's keyed CapTP session over a WebSocket to a peer that has a socket. Only bytes cross it; it holds no capability of yours.",
.date = @date("2026-07-21T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A browser opens no listening socket and dials no raw TCP or QUIC, so it has no
netlayer of its own. The relay only carries bytes
([ADR-0086](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0086-byte-transparent-relay.md)).

## Byte transparency

The keyed `op:start-session` handshake and every capability that follows it run
between the browser and the far peer. The relay never parses a frame and never
holds a capability; on a cleartext path it can still read every byte. Native
peers seal that stream with `noise-relay` when the intermediary must see only
ciphertext. A browser tab does not compose that provider, and uses WebRTC or
cleartext `keyed-relay` instead. The session's authority is the keys at its two
ends, so the relay carries a session without being party to it.

## Reaching the other side

A peer with a socket dials the address it wants. A browser has none, so the relay
reaches the far side two ways.

### Dialing a far peer

The browser names a target and the relay dials it. The reference relay reads
the target off the WebSocket's query: `?to=host:port` opens a TCP socket, and
`?locator=<ocapn-uri>` opens an iroh channel. The rooms app embeds the same
pump behind its own `/relay?target=<locator>` route and classifies the
locator through the same `pancake_host_plan_dial` a native dial uses; that
route is the app's, not the reference contract. Either way the relay then
pipes the WebSocket to whatever it dialed.

[The browser names the peer it wants; the relay dials it over tcp or iroh and pipes the two together. The keyed session runs end to end, and the relay only moves bytes.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-dial.svg'))

The dial policy is the embedding app's. The [rooms demo]($link.page('examples'))
pins its TCP target to the room server, so a classified TCP dial reaches the room
and nothing else. An iroh target may be any key-verified node, a wider reach the
app accepts and bounds with its own admission caps.

### Cold-dial rendezvous

When both ends are browsers, neither can be dialed. A browser registers its key with
the relay and leaves an inbound socket held there. A second browser, holding an
introduction that names that key, cold-dials it, and the relay bridges the dialer to
the held socket
([ADR-0088](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0088-fully-functioning-browser-peer.md)).
The room that made the introduction carries no part of the session.

[One browser registers and waits; the other redeems an introduction by cold-dialing that identity. The relay bridges the held socket to the dialer, and the keyed session runs end to end between the two browsers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-rendezvous.svg'))

Both paths run through one byte pump, so rendezvous and direct dialing share
their backpressure and teardown behavior.

## Bounded resources

A relay carries other peers' sessions, so it bounds what it holds. Backpressure on
both legs caps the bytes in flight: a stalled reader stops the relay reading its
partner, and the stall reaches the sender instead of accumulating in the
middle. An idle timeout reclaims silent sessions. A relayed connection costs
buffers but no CapTP session state. An embedding app adds its own ceilings on
the live-session and held-registration counts as admission policy, which the
[rooms demo]($link.page('examples')) sets. The relay counts bytes and sessions, never
messages, because it reads none.

## One contract, every runtime

The relay is a small contract an embedder runs in its own runtime.
`@pancake/relay` is the Deno reference; Python, Rust, and Racket provide the
same forwarding, backpressure, and idle-reclaim behavior.

## Not a netlayer

The relay *service* is not a netlayer. A dial dispatches on a
[locator]($link.page('concepts'))'s transport symbol, and the relay registers
none of those residents: not `tcp`, `keyed-tcp`, `noise-tcp`, `inproc`, `iroh`,
`webrtc`, or the others.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) lists them.

A `keyed-relay` locator names the waiting peer's key, not the relay. The
dialing host's configured relay URL is how that resident reaches the peer
([ADR-0088](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0088-fully-functioning-browser-peer.md)).
`noise-relay` uses the same pipe and seals the CapTP stream before it begins.
Peers dialing an ordinary netlayer never touch the relay service. The
[Noise guide]($link.page('transports/noise')) covers the sealed path.

## Dial

Choose `noise-relay` for an end-to-end sealed stream. Use `keyed-relay` only
when the relay and network are already trusted. Each tab below stops once the
transport-specific runtime and locator are ready; the binding page owns the
common connect, fetch, and call workflow.

